toyota was one of the first automakers  to embrace an easy future i mean how  could anyone forget the iconic toyota  prius the prius didn't have the best of  social images but it was and still is  one of the most cost-effective vehicles  on the road aside from pioneering the  way with hybrids toyota was also an  early investor in tesla way back in 2010  toyota invested 50 million dollars into  tesla which gave them a 1.43 take in the  company if they were still holding on to  this stake it would be worth over 10  billion dollars but unfortunately they  sold the stake in mid-2017  at the time it it seemed like toyota was  simply taking risk off the table and the  locking in profits  but looking back it seems like something  changed with toyota as they sold off  this stake since then toyota has  actually become more of an eevee opposer  for example toyota has actually been  lobbying to slow down the move to evs  they've also been behind the curve when  it comes to fully electric vehicles it  wasn't till april of 2022 that they  released their first fully electric suv  on the market and they've actually  recalled this vehicle due to the wheels  falling off so it's not exactly doing  great now supporters of toyota would  probably point out that toyota is  investing a significant amount into  battery technology for instance they  have a plan to invest 13.5 billion  dollars into battery tech by the end of  the decade but compared to the rest of  the industry this is rather  underwhelming take ford for example ford  is literally drowning in debt with over  200 billion dollars in liabilities yet  they just increased their ev investment  from 30 billion through 2026 to 50  billion dollars similarly volkswagen is  investing  and 180 billion dollars into ev usa over  the next four years considering these  investments toyota's commitment is  rather underwhelming clearly it they  haven't ditched evie's altogether but at  the same time they're also refusing to  go all in and jump onto the eevee  bandwagon so why is toyota so hesitant  with evs  one of the main reasons toyota has been  so hesitant when it comes to evs is  because they've been betting on hydrogen  according to toyota's vice chairman they  don't want to be seen as an ev maker but  as a carbon neutral company toyota  argues that this carbon neutral future  does not need to be achieved with evs  carbon neutrality is not about having a  single choice but about keeping options  open this in itself is not a bad stance  per se but thus far we've only seen  issues when it comes to hydrogen  vehicles take the 2021 tokyo olympics  for example toyota supply the olympics  with 500 hydrogen buses and cars the  vehicles themselves worked all right but  the same it could not be said about  their finances you see toyota leased out  these buses to japan for 900 000 for six  years a comparable diesel bus could have  been leased out for 220 000 for 15 years  on an annual basis the hydrogen bus  works out to 150 thousand dollars per  year while the diesel bus works out to  under 15 000 per year in other words the  hydrogen option is literally 10 times as  expensive and that's just the least  unlike evs where fueling up is  dramatically cheaper than gas  counterparts with these hydrogen buses  it's actually 2.6 times more expensive  if you thought evs were already  expensive these are clearly on a  different level to make things worse  financial concerns are just the first of  your worries hydrogen vehicles also  suffer from an extremely underdeveloped  refueling network unlike electric  charging you can't just set up a few  charging stations that link up their  local power grid instead you have to buy  hydrogen-specific fuel dispensers that  often cost a hundred thousand dollars  per unit you also have to spend a  significant amount on storing the  hydrogen and you also have to deal with  regular hydrogen deliveries in other  words you basically have to run a gas  station and given that the majority of  gas stations make their money off of the  convenience stores attached to the gas  stations i don't think they'll be  particularly thrilled to invest into  hydrogen infrastructure one advantage  that should be pointed out however is  that filling up a hydrogen vehicle is  similar to filling up a gas vehicle  meaning that you can get a full tank of  gas within just three to five minutes  if you're someone who does a lot of road  trips this is likely a significant  advantage but that's pretty much where  the advantages end and it's not just a  fueling process that's similar between  gas vehicles and hydrogen vehicles the  maintenance process is also quite  similar you see hydrogen vehicles are  way more complex than evs meaning that  they naturally come along with more  maintenance given that more things can  go wrong so hydrogen vehicles just don't  stack up against evs but then why is  toyota so determined to go down this  path well the answer seems to lie in  what's best for japan as opposed to  what's best for everyone else  japan's obsession with hydrogen is  nothing new  in fact it's just one of many avenues  that japan has been experimenting with  for decades  and the main reason the country does not  want to ban to western standards is  because of the country's energy  dependence japan is basically just an  island which means that they don't have  any neighboring countries at first  glance this might seem cool they have  this entire piece of land all to  themselves but logistically this is  actually a nightmare here's the thing  when you aren't physically attached to  any other countries shipping becomes  extremely difficult because everything  has to be shipped through the sea  and this is especially a problem when  we're talking about energy  you see it much of the world that either  creates their own energy or they rely on  neighboring countries  europe for example used to rely on  russia for crude oil and natural gas  they had a massive set of pipelines that  facilitated trade between these two  regions a similar case can be seen with  america as well canada is the largest  energy exporter to america and the  reasoning is obvious the two countries  are touching which makes it easy to  trade energy japan obviously does not  have any such partners they also don't  have any of the high demand resources  like crude oil or natural gas so they've  essentially been forced into importing  all of their energy this is not only  expensive but it also puts japan at a  massive trading disadvantage i mean when  you're importing at 94 percent of your  energy you're not exactly in the best  position to implement tariffs or start a  trade war the other countries could head  back with an energy boycott near country  would be screwed within a matter of days  japan is obviously not a fan of this  situation and they've been trying to  escape for decades  their initial solution to this conundrum  was going all-in on nuclear energy and  this was actually playing out really  well  at their peak in 2010 they were able to  produce 13 of the entire country's  energy needs using nuclear power but all  of this would come crashing down in 2011  with the fukushima nuclear disaster you  probably heard about this on the news  154 000 people had to be evacuated and  it's ranked as the second worst nuclear  disaster of all time only be nod by  chernobyl the worst part is that japan  couldn't have even done anything about  it it's not like the disaster was caused  by mismanagement or negligence it was  actually caused by a record-breaking  earthquake plus tsunami combination  really the only way japan could have  avoided this disaster was to not support  nuclear energy in the first place and  that's exactly what they've been trying  to do ever since but this has catapulted  the entire country into a perpetual  energy crisis nowadays it's extremely  common for japan to issue power  conservation messages to households and  companies as the country is constantly  fighting to avoid blackouts as you would  guess japan has been frantically looking  for solutions ever since the disaster  and the top solution they've landed on  it is hydrogen  while japan does not have access to any  of the common sources of energy what  they do have access to is hydrogen for  once japan's ocean surroundings are  actually helpful you see it the ocean  floor surrounding japan is filled with  the sherbet like substance often  referred to as fire ice  these ice crystals hold a tremendous  amount of natural methane which can be  converted into even more hydrogen it's  estimated that just one cubic meter of  methane hydrate can produce 160 cubic  meters of methane gas which can then be  turned into hydrogen and given that  japan has an exclusive economic zone  that stretches to both sides of the  country you have access to plenty of  fire ice  now it's not all sunshines and rainbows  given that it's not easy to extract fire  ice first you have to voyage deep into  the ocean where the seafloor is a  thousand meters under the surface from  there you have to drill hundreds of  meters into the seabed to actually  retrieve the fire ice japan has been  working out on a new drilling method  that can potentially extract fire ice  from shallower locations and then send  them through underwater pipelines but  this is still a work in progress  it's not clear if this new process will  work out but it doesn't matter whether  it does or not for the first time in a  long time japan actually has an  alternative form of energy and if this  process doesn't work out they're just  going to keep trying different processes  until they figure out something that  does work for them this is not a matter  of oh let's switch to renewable energy  because it's the right thing to do it  and it's trendy  now for them this is a matter of we have  to figure this out asap if we want to  save japan from the energy crisis  so they're obviously taking this  extremely seriously and as japan's  largest company toyota is obviously  doing everything they can to support  this division and yes that includes law  in other countries to slow down eevee  adoption when you look at it from the  western perspective it seems like toyota  is the bad guys who are teaming up with  the all giants like shell and exxon to  stall this wholesome movement towards  evs but that's not really the case at  all toyota isn't against renewable  energy or evs they're simply trying to  promote the form of energy that would be  most beneficial for their own country  global energy changes don't happen often  if anything they might happen once a  century so we're truly living through  historic times and japan only has one  shot at getting this right either they  convinced the world that hydrogen is  worth pursuing right now  or they get left behind as the entire  world switches to evs  if this happens japan would just become  even more dependent on energy imports  also allocating a significant portion of  their electricity towards running  electric cars will simply increase their  chances of a blackout even more so  that's the last thing japan wants to do  something else that we should note is  that japan is not even the country that  would benefit most from a switch to  hydrogen  it's not like japan has so much hydrogen  that they would become the next saudi  arabia or something rather a trend  towards hydrogen would simply alleviate  their dependency for energy the country  that would really profit from the switch  is china who is leader in hydrogen  production and consumption china's  current hydrogen usage comes in at a  whopping at 24 million tons per year now  it should be noted that most of this  hydrogen is great hydrogen meaning that  it's produced using fossil fuels but  china is setting up loads of green  hydrogen plants as we speak so they  would be the ones that would benefit  most from such a transition so in the  end why is toyota refusing to jump onto  the eevee bandwagon despite pioneering  the industry  well their home country japan has been  struggling with energy for decades and  there has recently been a glimmer of  hope with hydrogen so they're trying  their best to leverage this opportunity  and maximize the benefit for japan but  only time will tell it if this gamble  will be worth it now that you can see it  from japan's perspective do you think  toyota is doing the wrong thing comment  that down below  also drop a like if you hope that japan  is able to overcome their energy crisis  and of course consider checking out our  discord community to suggest after video  ideas and consider subscribing see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one

